  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and t-storms mainly for\ninterior Southern New England this afternoon through sundown. A\ncouple could produce small hail and gusty winds. \n\nMain concern for the rest of the afternoon into early tonight\nrevolves around low-topped showers and thunderstorms. A rather\npotent shortwave disturbance is visualized in water vapor\nimagery early this afternoon near Ottawa ON, forecast to move\nsoutheast through New England into early tonight.\nModest instability given dewpoints falling into the mid 50s is\npresent across most of New England, but stronger forcing and the\ncool pocket of air aloft due to the shortwave is contributing \nto scattered convective showers across NY and VT. BUFKIT soundings\nshow strong 925-700 mb lapse rates as well as strong mid-upper \nlevel flow aloft, but with low equilibrium levels around 25-28k \nand wetbulb zero heights on the order of 9000 ft. Have seen some\nlightning strikes in eastern Ontario, and anticipate some of\nthese showers could blossom into scattered t-storms. With the\ncold profiles aloft, even stronger showers could produce\ngraupel/\"dippin' dots\" hailstones, with deeper t-storms capable\nof hail up to 1\" diameter with locally strong/gusty winds. The\nsevere weather risk level has increased to a Level 1 of 5, but\nstill think the risk storms become severe is on the low side. The\nbest chance for storms is mainly from a FIT- ORH- PVD line \nsouth and west, with showers more likely into eastern MA. \nShowers/storms are expected to dissipate into the early-mid \nevening hours associated with sundown and passage of the \nshortwave disturbance. \n\nClearing skies overnight, which will bring a risk for patchy\nriver valley fog as winds go light northerly. A big airmass\nchange comes in tonight with much lower humidity levels and lows\nin the mid 50s to around 60, so a good night to crack open the\nwindows!\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasant weekend with seasonably cool\ntemperatures and comfortable humidities.\n\nBeautiful weekend shaping up as a 1024 mb high pressure\nestablishes itself over Southern New England Saturday, then\nbegins to move offshore into Sunday. Full sun, light northerly\nwinds and dewpoints falling into the 40s Saturday will bring a \ntaste of early-autumn, with highs in the mid 70s to around 80 as\n850 mb temps run around +7 to +10C. Very strong radiational \ncooling setup Saturday night, with widespread 50s for lows as \nridge of high pressure centers itself overhead, to go along with \npatchy river valley fog. A bit more of an increase in cloud \ncover Sunday, with risk for showers developing late in the day \non Sunday in western New England at earliest, but most of the \ndaytime hours are dry.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Rain chances return late Sunday into Monday, \nfollowed by quieter weather through most of next week.\n\nSunday starts off under mid-level ridging with heights falling \nthrough the day ahead of an approaching shortwave trough in the \nflow. This will keep conditions dry most of the day Sunday with \ntemperatures in the upper 70s/low 80s. There is general agreement \nacross most guidance in rain arriving Sunday night, spreading from \nwest to east. Expect batches of showers to continue through Monday. \nCan't rule out an embedded storm or downpour with marginal \ninstability around. With PWATs 1.5-1.8\", brief heavier rain rates \nare possible. Mid-level winds will be strong enough to keep any \nheavier showers or storms moving if there are any. This should help \nminimize the risk for flash flooding. Can't rule out isolated \ninstances of flash flood impacts in poor drainage/urban areas given \nthe potenial for embedded heavier downpours. This has been reflected \nin the WPC ERO as a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4). For total amounts, \nensemble guidance shows a range of 0.25\" to 0.50\" across southern \nNew England. Localized higher amounts are possible, especially \nwithin any downpours. \n\nA cold front moves through the region by Tuesday with a drier \nairmass advecting into southern New England behind it. Conditions \nremain generally dry Tuesday and Wednesday with zonal flow aloft. \nTemperatures will likely be in seasonable ranges in the upper 70s to \nlow 80s with dewpoints in the low to mid 60s keeping humidity \nleaning toward the more comfortable side. Ensemble guidance signal \nfor a trend toward unsettled weather by late-week with chances for \nrain returning to the forecast.",
